honoring a family that's lost so much a  week after seven members of the  zacharias family were killed in a mass  casualty crash in north las vegas clark  county leaders have come together to  honor them and speak out against  speeding on our roadways 8 news now  reporter madison kimbrough joins us with  more on tonight's event  yeah this caravan vigil which started  around 4 p.m has had a steady flow of  people and cars drive through as you can  see behind me and they have arrived here  at the crash site on cheyenne and  commerce drivers met up at the cheyenne  sports complex around 4pm and then drove  as a group to cheyenne and commerce  north las vegas mayor john lee along  with councilman isaac brown in  attendance trying to bring awareness to  speeding across the valley as this is a  chronic problem on our roadways people  could be seen wearing red shirts saying  stop speed accompanied by the north las  vegas fire and police department as of  wednesday morning the zacharias family  disabled their gofundme page after  reaching their goal of 300 thousand  dollars the mother and sister of the  victims her linda zacharias thanked the  community for their support we spoke to  a man who knows the family directly and  while he is giving the family space to  grieve he wanted to come out today and  show his support this way it's just so  hard  because we've seen the kids we've met  the kids and  what the family is going through right  now is just  there's no words there's no words to  explain but we just want to be here and  show our presence and support them that  we're here for them this isn't the only  vigil happening for the zacharias family  another one will be held tomorrow at the  mlk statue in north las vegas that's  happening from 4 30 to 6 p.m both vigils  are open to the public  [Music]  we will be out here all evening giving  you the latest details if you are headed  this way make sure to take it slow as  traffic is heavy reporting from shine in  commerce ma